35
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:52,817
downstairs How brave they’ll all think me at home!

36
00:02:52,818 --> 00:02:57,377
Why, I wouldn’t say anything about it, even if I fell off the top of the house!”

37
00:02:57,378 --> 00:02:59,718
(Which was very likely true.)

38
00:02:59,719 --> 00:03:05,119
Down, down, down with the fall never come to an end?

39
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:10,038
“I wonder how many miles I’ve fallen by this time?” she said aloud. “I must be

40
00:03:10,039 --> 00:03:12,679
getting somewhere near the center of the earth.

41
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:17,538
Let me see: that would be four thousand miles down, I think—”

42
00:03:17,539 --> 00:03:22,038
(for, you see, Alice had learned several things of this sort in her lessons in

43
00:03:22,039 --> 00:03:26,359
the school room and though this was not a very good opportunity for showing off

44
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:31,157
her knowledge, as there was no one to listen to her, still it was good practice

45
00:03:31,158 --> 00:03:37,698
to say it over) “—yes, that’s about the right distance— —but then I wonder what Latitude or

46
00:03:37,699 --> 00:03:42,619
Longitude I’ve got to?” (Alice had no idea what Latitude was, or

47
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:48,138
Longitude either, but thought they were nice grand words to say.)

48
00:03:48,139 --> 00:03:50,359
Presently she began again.

49
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:54,977
“I wonder if I shall fall right through the earth! How funny it’ll seem to come

50
00:03:54,978 --> 00:03:58,398
out among the people that walk with their heads downward!

51
00:03:58,399 --> 00:04:03,739
The Antipathies, I think—” (she was rather glad there was no one

52
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:08,477
listening, this time, as it didn’t sound at all the right word) “—but I shall have

53
00:04:08,478 --> 00:04:11,539
to ask them what the name of the country is, you know.

54
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:17,119
Please, Ma’am, is this New Zealand or Australia?” (and she tried to curtsy as she

55
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:22,159
spoke— —fancy curtsying as you’re falling through the air! Do you think you could

56
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:27,799
manage it?) “And what an ignorant little girl she’ll think me No, it’ll never do

57
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:31,638
to ask: perhaps I shall see it written up somewhere.”

58
00:04:31,639 --> 00:04:38,059
Down, down, down. There was nothing else to do, so Alice soon began talking again

59
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:43,219
Dino miss me very much tonight I should think!” (Dinah was the cat.)

60
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:46,958
“I hope they’ll remember her saucer of milk at tea time

61
00:04:46,959 --> 00:04:49,638
Dynamite dear! I wish you were down here

62
00:04:49,639 --> 00:04:52,578
with me! There are no mice in the air, I’m

63
00:04:52,579 --> 00:04:57,559
afraid, but you might catch a bat, and there's very likely a mouse, you know. But

64
00:04:57,560 --> 00:05:01,878
do cats eat bats, I wonder?” And here Alice began to get rather

65
00:05:01,879 --> 00:05:08,958
sleepy, and went on saying to herself, in a dreamy sort of way, “Do cats eat bats?

66
00:05:08,959 --> 00:05:17,119
Do cats eat bats?” and sometimes, “Do bats eat cats?” for, you see, as she couldn’t answer

67
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:21,138
either question, it didn’t much matter which way she put it.

68
00:05:21,139 --> 00:05:25,638
She felt that she was dozing off, and had just begun to dream that she was walking

69
00:05:25,639 --> 00:05:30,917
hand in hand with Dinah, and saying to her very earnestly, “Now, Dinah, tell me the

70
00:05:30,918 --> 00:05:35,958
truth: did you ever eat a bat?” when suddenly, thump!

71
00:05:35,959 --> 00:05:37,159
thump!

72
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:44,119
down she came upon a heap of sticks and dry leaves, and the fall was over.

73
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:49,818
Alice was not a bit hurt, and she jumped up onto her feet in a moment: she looked

74
00:05:49,819 --> 00:05:55,758
up, but it was all dark overhead; before her was another long passage, and

75
00:05:55,759 --> 00:06:00,977
the White Rabbit was still in sight, hurrying down it. There was not a moment

76
00:06:00,978 --> 00:06:04,219
to be lost: away went Alice like the wind,

77
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:09,499
and was just in time to hear it say, as it turned a corner, “Oh my ears and

78
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:13,999
whiskers, how late it’s getting!” She was close behind it when she turned

79
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,838
the corner, but the Rabbit was no longer to be seen:

80
00:06:17,839 --> 00:06:22,398
she found herself in a long, low hall, which was lit up by a row of lamps

81
00:06:22,399 --> 00:06:24,078
hanging from the roof.

82
00:06:24,079 --> 00:06:29,778
There were doors all around the hall, but they were all locked; and when Alice had

83
00:06:29,779 --> 00:06:35,477
been all the way down one side and up the other, trying every door, she walked

84
00:06:35,478 --> 00:06:40,518
sadly down the middle, wondering how she was ever to get out again.

85
00:06:40,519 --> 00:06:46,099
Suddenly she came upon a little three-legged table, all made of solid glass;

86
00:06:46,100 --> 00:06:52,578
there was nothing on it except a tiny golden key, and Alice’s first thought was that it

87
00:06:52,579 --> 00:06:58,938
might belong to one of the doors of the hall; but, alas! either the locks were too large,

88
00:06:58,939 --> 00:07:05,299
or the key was too small, but at any rate it would not open any of them.

89
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:09,917
However the second time around she came upon a low curtain she had not noticed

90
00:07:09,918 --> 00:07:16,037
before, and behind it was a little door about 15 inches high: she tried the

91
00:07:16,038 --> 00:07:21,018
little golden key in the lock, and to her great delight it fitted!

92
00:07:21,019 --> 00:07:26,659
Alice opened the door and found that it led into a small passage, not much larger

93
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:31,338
than a rat hole she knelt down and looked along the passage into the

94
00:07:31,339 --> 00:07:33,917
loveliest garden you ever saw.

95
00:07:33,918 --> 00:07:39,739
How she longed to get out of that dark hall, and wander about among those beds

96
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:45,258
of bright flowers and those cool fountains, but she could not even get her head

97
00:07:45,259 --> 00:07:49,518
through the doorway; “and even if my head would go through,”

98
00:07:49,519 --> 00:07:54,499
thought poor Alice, “it would be a very little use without my shoulders.

99
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:57,917
Oh have I wish I could shut up like a telescope!

100
00:07:57,918 --> 00:08:02,059
I think I could, if I only knew how to begin.”

101
00:08:02,060 --> 00:08:06,679
For, you see, so many out of the way things had happened lately, that Alice

102
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:11,718
had begun to think that very few things indeed were really impossible.

103
00:08:11,719 --> 00:08:16,457
There seemed to be no use in waiting by the little door, so she went back to the

104
00:08:16,458 --> 00:08:22,037
table, half hoping she might find another key on it, or at any rate a book of rules

105
00:08:22,038 --> 00:08:26,719
for shutting people up like telescopes: this time she found a little bottle on

106
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:32,298
it, (“which certainly was not there before,” said Alice,) and round the neck of the

107
00:08:32,299 --> 00:08:38,599
bottle was a paper label, with the words “DRINK ME,” beautifully printed on it in

108
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:44,418
large letters. It was all very well to say “Drink me,” but the wise little Alice

109
00:08:44,419 --> 00:08:46,957
was not going to do that in a hurry.

110
00:08:46,958 --> 00:08:52,999
“No, I’ll look first,” she said, “and see whether it’s marked poison or not”; for

111
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,719
she had read several nice little histories about children who had got

112
00:08:56,720 --> 00:09:02,538
burnt, and eaten up by wild beasts and other unpleasant things, all because they

113
00:09:02,539 --> 00:09:07,158
would not remember the simple rules their friends had taught them: such as,

114
00:09:07,159 --> 00:09:11,599
that a red-hot poker will burn you if you hold it too long; and that if you cut

115
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:16,819
your finger very deeply with a knife, it usually bleeds; and she had never

116
00:09:16,820 --> 00:09:21,319
forgotten that, if you drink much from a bottle marked “poison,” it is almost

117
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:24,918
certain to disagree with you, sooner or later.

118
00:09:24,919 --> 00:09:31,277
However, this bottle was not marked “poison,” so Alice ventured to taste it, and

119
00:09:31,278 --> 00:09:36,079
finding it very nice, (it had, in fact, a sort of mixed flavor of cherry tart

120
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:40,038
custard pineapple roast turkey, toffee, and hot buttered toast,)

121
00:09:40,039 --> 00:09:46,579
she very soon finished it off. “What a curious feeling!” said Alice; “I must be

122
00:09:46,580 --> 00:09:52,338
shutting up like a telescope.” And so it was indeed: she was now only 10 inches

123
00:09:52,339 --> 00:09:57,199
high, and her face brightened up at the thought that she was now the right size

124
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:02,119
for going through the little door into that lovely garden. First, however, she

125
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:06,499
waited for a few minutes to see if she was going to shrink any further:

126
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:12,018
she felt a little nervous about this; “for it might end, you know,” said Alice to

127
00:10:12,019 --> 00:10:15,798
herself, “in my going out altogether, like a candle.

128
00:10:15,799 --> 00:10:20,957
I wonder what I should be like then?” And she tried to fancy with a flame of a

129
00:10:20,958 --> 00:10:25,338
candle is like after the candle is blown out, for she could not remember ever

130
00:10:25,339 --> 00:10:27,319
having seen such a thing.

131
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:33,798
After a while, finding that nothing more happened, she decided on going into the garden at once;

132
00:10:33,799 --> 00:10:39,678
but, alas for poor Alice! when she got to the door, she found she had forgotten the

133
00:10:39,679 --> 00:10:44,777
little golden key, and when she went back to the table for it, she found she could

134
00:10:44,778 --> 00:10:48,739
not possibly reach it: she could see it quite plainly through

135
00:10:48,740 --> 00:10:53,599
the glass, and she tried her best to climb up one of the legs of the table,

136
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:57,739
but it was too slippery; and when she had tired herself out with

137
00:10:57,740 --> 00:11:02,719
trying, the poor little thing sat down and cried.

138
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:08,898
“Come, there’s no use in crying like that!” said Alice to herself, rather sharply; “I

139
00:11:08,899 --> 00:11:13,219
advise you to leave off this minute!” She generally gave herself very good

140
00:11:13,220 --> 00:11:18,439
advice, (though she very seldom followed it), and sometimes she scolded herself so

141
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:24,798
severely as to bring tears into her eyes; and once she remembered trying to box

142
00:11:24,799 --> 00:11:31,038
her own ears for having cheated herself in a game of croquet she was playing against herself,

143
00:11:31,039 --> 00:11:36,138
for this curious child was very fond of pretending to be two people.

144
00:11:36,139 --> 00:11:41,719
“But it’s no use now,” thought poor Alice, “to pretend to be two people! Why, there’s

145
00:11:41,720 --> 00:11:45,678
hardly enough of me left to make one respectable person!”

146
00:11:45,679 --> 00:11:51,319
Soon her eye fell on a little glass box that was lying under the table: she

147
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:58,699
opened it, and found in it a very small cake, on which the words “EAT ME” were

148
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:04,878
beautifully marked in currents Well, I’ll eat it,” said Alice, “and if it

149
00:12:04,879 --> 00:12:09,979
makes me grow larger, I can reach the key; and if it makes me grow smaller, I can

150
00:12:09,980 --> 00:12:14,957
creep under the door; so either way I’ll get into the garden, and I don’t care

151
00:12:14,958 --> 00:12:20,898
which happens!” She ate a little bit, and said anxiously to herself, “Which way?

152
00:12:20,899 --> 00:12:25,939
Which way?”, holding her hand on the top of her head to feel which way it was

153
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:32,298
growing, and she was quite surprised to find that she remained the same size: to

154
00:12:32,299 --> 00:12:37,099
be sure, this generally happens when one eats a cake, but Alice had got so much

155
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:41,479
into the way of expecting nothing but out of the way things to happen, that it

156
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:47,298
seemed quite dull and stupid for life to go on in the common way. So she set to

157
00:12:47,299 --> 00:12:51,000
work, and very soon finished off the cake
